      "answer": "Popular participation gives government democratic legitimacy because citizens have a role in choosing representatives and shaping public authority. However, participation by itself cannot prevent abuse. Constitutional limits define the powers of institutions, protect important rights, require public authorities to act according to law and create mechanisms through which decisions can be challenged. Equal citizenship also means that a temporary majority cannot simply remove the basic protections of other citizens. Thus constitutional democracy combines popular rule with rules and institutions that keep power accountable."
